{
  "scenario": {
    "id": "P11",
    "title": "ISA comparison - the same benchmark under the arm_like and riscv_like cost profiles",
    "layer": "1 ISA",
    "description": "The same checksum loop as P10 (in SRAM, to keep the memory hierarchy out of the picture) is charged with two illustrative cost tables. arm_like has load/store multiple and 16-bit encodings for simple operations; riscv_like models base RV32IMC without Zcmp: no store/load-multiple (one instruction per register; Hazard3 adds Zcmp's cm.push/cm.popret, which this profile leaves out), compare-and-branch instructions, and mostly 4-byte encodings. The summary reports cycles, instructions retired, CPI and static code size per profile. The numbers are illustrative models, not measurements of real cores.\n",
    "program": "bench",
    "options": {
      "iterations": 200,
      "code_region": "sram"
    },
    "config": {
      "seed": 1,
      "max_cycles": 400000
    },
    "variants": [
      {
        "name": "arm_like",
        "config": {
          "profile": "arm_like"
        }
      },
      {
        "name": "riscv_like",
        "config": {
          "profile": "riscv_like"
        }
      }
    ],
    "expected": {
      "summary": "Both profiles execute the same program; they differ in instret (riscv_like retires fewer because it has compare-and-branch instructions, but more for push/pop), in cycles and CPI, and in static code size (arm_like denser).",
      "checks": [
        {
          "check": "no_faults",
          "variant": "arm_like"
        },
        {
          "check": "no_faults",
          "variant": "riscv_like"
        },
        {
          "check": "variant_lt",
          "metric": "bench.static_code_bytes",
          "a": "arm_like",
          "b": "riscv_like"
        },
        {
          "check": "variant_lt",
          "metric": "bench.bench_cycles",
          "a": "arm_like",
          "b": "riscv_like"
        },
        {
          "check": "variant_lt",
          "metric": "bench.instret",
          "a": "riscv_like",
          "b": "arm_like"
        }
      ]
    },
    "what_to_look_for": "summary.variants.arm_like.bench vs summary.variants.riscv_like.bench: bench_cycles, instret, cpi and static_code_bytes. The static instruction count (16) is the same because the source is the same; static_code_bytes differs because of the encodings (Thumb-2 16-bit forms vs 32-bit with some compressed forms). instret differs because RISC-V has compare-and-branch instructions (cmp+bne becomes one bne) while base RV32IMC without Zcmp needs five stores for push {r4-r7, lr}. CPI is cycles divided by instret and so moves in the opposite direction to instret. The \"bench\" CLI command prints this as a table.\n",
    "likely_root_causes": [
      "A real ARM vs RISC-V difference in code size usually comes from Thumb-2's dense 16-bit encodings and load/store multiple; RISC-V narrows it with the C extension, and on Hazard3 with Zcmp (cm.push/cm.popret) when the compiler uses it.",
      "A difference in instruction count comes from ISA features: compare-and-branch instructions and no condition flags on RISC-V, multi-register push/pop on ARM (base RV32IMC has no store-multiple).",
      "A CPI difference on the real board also includes pipeline depth, branch prediction and memory latency, which this model does not have."
    ],
    "how_to_debug": [
      "On the board: build the same C source for both cores with -O2 and compare 'size' output (text bytes) and the objdump instruction counts.",
      "Read DWT CYCCNT on the Cortex-M33 and mcycle/minstret on Hazard3 around the same loop; compute CPI = cycles / instructions for each.",
      "Vary the benchmark (load/store heavy vs branch heavy) and explain each change in terms of an ISA feature, not a guess.",
      "Subtract the counter read overhead on each core before comparing."
    ],
    "what_this_means": "Instructions, cycles and bytes are three different measures and an ISA can win one while losing another. Cycles per instruction is the bridge between them; comparing only wall time hides which architectural feature made the difference. This simulation shows the shape of the comparison; the real board gives the numbers.\n",
    "hidden_symptom": "The RISC-V build is larger and retires fewer instructions for the same C code."
  },
